            0.358
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "11.4 OHM’S LAW"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.367,
            0.722,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "**Is there a relationship between the potential difference across a conductor and the current through it? Let us explore with an Activity.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.134,
            0.411,
            0.302,
            0.432
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 11.1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.124,
            0.442,
            0.506,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": "**Set up a circuit as shown in Fig. 11.2, consisting of a nichrome wire XY of length, say 0.5 m, an ammeter, a voltmeter and four cells of 1.5 V each. (Nichrome is an alloy of nickel, chromium, manganese, and iron metals.)**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.53,
            0.506,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": "**First use only one cell as the source in the circuit. Note the reading in the ammeter I, for the current and reading of the voltmeter V for the potential difference across the nichrome wire XY in the circuit. Tabulate them in the Table given.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.511,
            0.407,
            0.915,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.518,
                0.596,
                0.913,
                0.611
              ],
              "content": "Figure 11.2 Electric circuit for studying Ohm’s law"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_4_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.617,
            0.507,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "n **Next connect two cells in the circuit and**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.63,
            0.898,
            0.661
          ],
          "continues_prev": true,
          "content": "**note the respective readings of the ammeter and voltmeter for the values of current through the nichrome wire and potential difference across the nichrome wire.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.661,
            0.868,
            0.675
          ],
          "content": "n **Repeat the above steps using three cells and then four cells in the circuit separately.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.675,
            0.819,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": "n **Calculate the ratio of V to I for each pair of potential difference V and current I.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.156,
            0.693,
            0.868,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": "| S. No. | Number of cells used in the circuit (ampere) | Current through the nichrome wire, I wire, V (volt) | Potential difference across the nichrome | V/I(volt/ampere) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | 1 |  |  |  |\n| 2 | 2 |  |  |  |\n| 3 | 3 |  |  |  |\n| 4 | 4 |  |  |  |",
